    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some trouble with these walnut dovetails i'm cutting for a cabinet set. the pins are splintering a bit at the very tips when i try to pare them down to size. i've checked my chisels and they feel sharp, but i keep getting these tiny little crushed fibers instead of a clean cut. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my grain management or tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splintering walnut dovetails, especially when paring down the pins, is a common frustration. It sounds like your chisel might be meeting the grain at an angle that's too aggressive for the delicate nature of the wood, or perhaps the grain itself is more knotty than you anticipated. Try adjusting your chisel angle slightly—a very fine, almost shaving-like bevel can sometimes coax a cleaner cut out of the denser wood. Also, try paring with the grain as much as possible, even if it means taking smaller, more controlled shavings.",
