    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i tried taking even thinner shavings but it's still happening on some of the more figured sections. could it be a moisture thing? the walnut has been sitting in the shop for a bit, but it feels pretty stable. also, do you think the bevel angle of the chisel itself matters, or should i just be looking at how i'm holding it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Moisture content can certainly play a role, especially with figured wood, which can have more internal stresses. If the wood is slightly too dry or too wet, it can become brittle or gummy, leading to those tiny crushed fibers instead of a clean shear. You should absolutely consider the bevel angle; it's not just about how you hold the tool, but the geometry of the tool itself. A slightly more acute bevel on the chisel can sometimes bite into the grain more cleanly, reducing the tearing action.",
